1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,683
This is Regista.

2
00:00:12,082 --> 00:00:14,410
Some graphics need no AI at all.

3
00:00:14,510 --> 00:00:21,082
Pass difficulty, top speed, shot speed and expected goals are computed by code, the instant they happen.

4
00:00:22,032 --> 00:00:26,919
When the game shifts, code notices first, and a template goes on air at once.

5
00:00:27,019 --> 00:00:34,855
Behind it, six agents on Microsoft Agent Framework and Foundry go to work, reading numbers only through metrics tools over MCP.

6
00:00:34,955 --> 00:00:39,566
The Verifier recomputes every number before the verified card replaces the template.

7
00:00:39,666 --> 00:00:45,415
Then the frame holds, like a TV replay, and the pitch shows why, drawn from the match data.

8
00:00:46,332 --> 00:00:52,934
The same facts, told for different viewers: an analyst in English, a casual fan in Italian following a favourite player.

9
00:00:53,034 --> 00:00:55,164
The numbers and the seal never change.

10
00:00:56,132 --> 00:00:59,282
In the control room, you watch the agents check each other.

11
00:00:59,382 --> 00:01:07,796
Here the Analyst credited two players who had already been substituted. The Verifier rejected it, and only the corrected version went on air.

12
00:01:08,732 --> 00:01:13,400
We measured it on 20 unseen matches with hidden storylines the AI never sees.

13
00:01:13,500 --> 00:01:17,097
It found about half of them, with a precision of 43 %,

14
00:01:17,197 --> 00:01:22,607
and not a single unsupported number went on air. Every miss is in the public report.

15
00:01:23,207 --> 00:01:32,446
It runs on Azure Container Apps, Microsoft Foundry, PostgreSQL, Blob Storage and Application Insights; the voice is Azure AI Speech.

16
00:01:33,046 --> 00:01:37,162
Regista. Reads the game, directs the broadcast.
